Database migrations (Alembic, via Flask-Migrate)
================================================

Single-database configuration.

How the schema is created
-------------------------
A fresh install runs `flask init-db`, which builds the tables directly with
create_all() and then stamps this migration history at "head" - so Alembic
knows the database is already current and will not try to re-create tables
that exist.

An existing install applies schema changes with:

    FLASK_APP=wsgi.py flask db upgrade

Always take a backup first (deploy/backup.sh).

Making a schema change
----------------------
1. Edit the model in app/models/.
2. Generate a revision:

       FLASK_APP=wsgi.py flask db migrate -m "what changed"

3. Read the generated file in versions/ before committing it. Autogenerate is
   good at added and removed columns, and unreliable about renames, server
   defaults and constraint changes - those usually need editing by hand.
4. Test it against a copy of a real database, both up and down:

       flask db upgrade
       flask db downgrade

Useful commands
---------------
    flask db current      which revision the database is on
    flask db history      the full revision graph
    flask db heads        the latest revision(s)
    flask db stamp head   mark the database current without running anything

SQLite note
-----------
SQLite cannot ALTER most column properties. Flask-Migrate handles this with
batch mode (it rebuilds the table), which the generated scripts use where
needed - but it is worth checking the generated script when a migration
changes an existing column rather than adding one.
